<body class="ph-body">
  <%- include('../partials/nav') %>

  <section class="ph-page-hero">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <div class="ph-eyebrow">Guide · Tactics</div>
      <h1 class="ph-page-title">Train the Recommendation Engine</h1>
      <p class="ph-page-lede">PodHaven's rec engine is a model that learns from <em>your</em> ratings. The more deliberate the input, the sharper the output. Here's how to feed it well.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <h2 class="ph-section-title">The four buttons, what they actually mean</h2>
      <p class="ph-lede">Three of them train the model. The fourth doesn't.</p>
      <ul class="ph-checklist ph-checklist-wide">
        <li><strong>Love</strong> — strong positive training signal. Boosts similar episodes hard. Use for episodes you'd recommend to a friend.</li>
        <li><strong>Like</strong> — gentle positive training signal. Use for episodes you enjoyed but wouldn't necessarily flag as standout.</li>
        <li><strong>Dislike</strong> — negative training signal. Pushes similar episodes down in future recommendations. Use for episodes that disappointed.</li>
        <li><strong>Not Interested</strong> — a <em>view filter</em>, not a training signal. The episode leaves the recommendation candidate pool, but the model doesn't learn anything from the rating. Use it to dismiss specific episodes you don't want recommended; use <strong>Dislike</strong> if you want the rec engine to actually move away from a topic.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="ph-tip">
        <strong>Common mix-up:</strong> "Not Interested" doesn't tell the model to avoid this kind of thing — it just hides this one episode. If the rec engine keeps surfacing a topic you've moved past, you want <strong>Dislike</strong> on a few of those episodes. That's the rating that actually trains.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section ph-section-alt">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <h2 class="ph-section-title">Where to rate from</h2>
      <p class="ph-lede">Three places, all equivalent — pick the one that fits your hands at the moment.</p>
      <ul class="ph-checklist ph-checklist-wide">
        <li><strong>Episode detail page.</strong> Tap the rating icon in the top toolbar. Gives you the full menu.</li>
        <li><strong>Long-press context menu.</strong> Long-press any episode in any list (Up Next, Episodes, podcast detail, recommendations) for a context menu with all four ratings inline.</li>
        <li><strong>Expanded Play Bar.</strong> While listening, tap the chevron-up to expand the Play Bar sheet. Rating is right there.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <h2 class="ph-section-title">A 10-minute training routine</h2>
      <p class="ph-lede">If you're brand-new to the rec engine, this is the fastest path to useful recommendations.</p>
      <ol class="ph-steps">
        <li>Open a podcast you know well. Scan the episode list.</li>
        <li>Find five episodes you remember loving — long-press, tap <strong>Love</strong>.</li>
        <li>Find three episodes that disappointed you — long-press, tap <strong>Dislike</strong>. (These are the negative training signals that actually shape recommendations.)</li>
        <li>Repeat across 2–3 podcasts. Total: ~15 ratings.</li>
        <li>Open Up Next. Scroll past your queue to the <strong>Recommended</strong> section. The picks should already feel like they know you.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>From there, just rate as you listen. Two or three ratings a day is plenty. Use <strong>Not Interested</strong> as a clean-up tool when you want a specific recommendation off your list — just remember it doesn't teach the model anything.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section ph-section-alt">
    <div class="ph-container ph-feature">
      <div class="ph-feature-copy">
        <h2 class="ph-section-title">Reading the reasons-why</h2>
        <p>Open a recommended episode's detail page. At the top you'll see a <strong>Recommendation score</strong> (a percentage), and beneath it up to three tags explaining why it surfaced.</p>
        <ul class="ph-checklist ph-checklist-wide">
          <li><strong>Similar to others.</strong> The episode looks semantically close to ones you've rated up.</li>
          <li><strong>Preferred podcast.</strong> The show itself has accumulated positive ratings.</li>
          <li><strong>Fresh.</strong> A recency boost — calibrated to the show's natural cadence.</li>
          <li><strong>Recommendation score.</strong> The number at the top tells you how strongly the pick lands. Use it to triage: high-percentage picks are safe bets; lower ones are experiments.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="ph-tip">The score and tags disappear once you rate or finish an episode — at that point your own verdict outranks the engine's guess, so there's nothing left to predict.</div>
      </div>
      <figure class="ph-feature-shot">
        <div class="ph-phone">
          <div class="ph-phone-screen">
            <img src="/public/images/podhaven/recommended-episode-detail.png" alt="A recommended episode's detail page: a Recommendation score of 58% above the chips Similar to others, Preferred podcast, and Fresh." loading="lazy">
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <h2 class="ph-section-title">Course-correcting</h2>
      <p>If the rec engine drifts somewhere you don't want it to go, fix it explicitly. The model responds quickly.</p>
      <ul class="ph-checklist ph-checklist-wide">
        <li><strong>Wrong topic surfacing repeatedly?</strong> <strong>Dislike</strong> two or three of those episodes — that's the rating that trains the model. <em>Not Interested</em> only hides the specific episodes you tap it on; it won't move the engine away from the topic.</li>
        <li><strong>Show recommended too aggressively?</strong> <strong>Dislike</strong> a few of its episodes. The base score is what drives whether the show appears at all, and Dislike is the rating that pulls that score down. (Resist the urge to reach for Freshness Cadence here — cadence controls <em>which episodes from the show feel fresh</em>, not <em>how often the show appears</em>.)</li>
        <li><strong>Show neglected unfairly?</strong> Rate three or four episodes as Like / Love. The positive signal lifts the whole show.</li>
        <li><strong>Old episodes for an evergreen show being skipped?</strong> Set its <a class="ph-inline-link" href="/podhaven/guides/per-podcast-settings#freshness">Freshness Cadence</a> to <strong>Evergreen</strong>. The freshness boost stops decaying with age, so a 2018 episode and a 2026 episode compete on the base score alone.</li>
        <li><strong>News show resurfacing 5-day-old episodes as "fresh"?</strong> Set its Freshness Cadence to <strong>Daily</strong>, so the freshness boost decays within roughly a day and only the very newest episodes get the boost.</li>
        <li><strong>Just want a specific recommendation off your list?</strong> That's exactly what <strong>Not Interested</strong> is for. Hides this episode without retraining anything.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="ph-tip">
        <strong>Mental model for the freshness knob:</strong> the rec engine's score for an episode is a <em>base score</em> from your ratings plus a <em>freshness boost</em> that decays over time. Cadence sets the decay rate. Ratings move the base score. To change whether a show is recommended, change ratings — not cadence.
      </div>
      <div class="ph-tip">
        <strong>Partial-listens count too.</strong> PodHaven tracks listening coverage as a bitmap — if you bail on an episode three minutes in, that's a soft negative signal even without a rating. So don't worry about "skewing the model" by abandoning a bad episode early.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section ph-section-alt">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <h2 class="ph-section-title">A note on privacy</h2>
      <p>Everything described here happens on your phone. The model lives on your device. Your ratings, your listening history, the embedding math — none of it leaves PodHaven. There's no server-side profile being built about you, no cohort the engine is comparing you to.</p>
      <p>That's why "train it on your taste" is the operating instruction, not a marketing line.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%- include('../partials/footer') %>
